The Korean War, also known as the Forgotten War, was a brutal conflict that lasted for three years from 1950 to 1953. While the war was fought between North and South Korea, many South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and the North's involvement was purely defensive.

One of the main arguments made by these experts is that the North Korean government was not interested in starting a war with South Korea. In fact, the North Korean government had been trying to establish a socialist state and had been focusing on building up its economy and infrastructure. However, the South Korean government, led by Syngman Rhee, had been trying to reunify the country and had been pushing for a more capitalist economy.

Another argument made by these experts is that the North Korean army was not prepared for a full-scale war. The North Korean army was still in the process of rebuilding and modernizing its military after the Japanese occupation, and it did not have the resources or the military capabilities to launch a full-scale war against South Korea.

